      "possibleRetry": false,
      "inputTokens": 136,
      "outputTokens": 1022,
      "costUsd": 0.009266,
      "pronunciationText": "Here's a formula that bends both ways too: y equals x cubed, minus six x squared, plus nine x. Its derivative, f prime, is a function in its own right. It gives the gradient at every x, so we can differentiate it again. The result is called the second derivative. It's written f double prime of x, or d two y by d x squared, or y double prime. Here, f prime of x is three x squared, minus twelve x, plus nine. Differentiate again, and f double prime of x is six x minus twelve. It tells us how fast the gradient is changing, and in which direction."
    },
