      "possibleRetry": false,
      "inputTokens": 176,
      "outputTokens": 1376,
      "costUsd": 0.012471999999999999,
      "pronunciationText": "Watch the tangent ride along the curve, with the gradient graph underneath. On the left, the gradient is falling, so the tangent keeps turning clockwise, and the curve bends down. That's called concave down. The curve sits below its tangents, like a cap. And concave down isn't the same as decreasing. This part rises, then falls, and it bends down the whole time. On the right, the gradient is rising, so the tangent turns anticlockwise, and the curve bends up. That's concave up. The curve sits above its tangents, like a cup. Now, f double prime is the gradient of this gradient graph. Where f prime is falling, f double prime is negative. Where f prime is rising, it's positive. So f double prime greater than zero means concave up, and less than zero means concave down."
    },
