      "possibleRetry": false,
      "inputTokens": 128,
      "outputTokens": 1004,
      "costUsd": 0.0091,
      "pronunciationText": "At x equals two, the gradient stops falling and starts rising. Its lowest value is minus three, right here. So the curve switches from concave down to concave up. A point where the concavity changes is called a point of inflection. It's at two, and f of two is two, so the point is two, two. Notice the tangent there crosses the curve. And it isn't flat. The gradient is minus three, so this inflection isn't a stationary point. The vase works the same way. Its level rises slowest at the widest part, and that's exactly where its graph switches bend."
    },
